WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:15.120
1916, only 13 years after the Wright brothers&nbsp;
flew the first heavier-than-air aircraft,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:20.160
an American company was established, ready to&nbsp;
revolutionize the fledgling aviation industry.
 &nbsp;

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:24.780
Founded as Pacific Aero Products and&nbsp;
renamed just a year later as Boeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.560
the company enjoyed decades of success and&nbsp;
developed some of the best technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:32.700
and many of the best aircraft of the time.
 
From the first modern commercial airliner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:40.140
the Boeing 247, launched in 1933, to the iconic&nbsp;
Jumbo 747, Boeing was able to amaze humankind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:48.300
for decades... until Airbus came along that is.
90 years after the launch of the iconic Boeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.280
247, the world of aviation today is radically&nbsp;
different. Not only does Boeing no longer lead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.720
the market, it has been overtaken by a&nbsp;
competitor almost half its age: Airbus.
 &nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.540
Take a look at this graph.
What you see is a hard-fought war,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:06.000
a market divided between two giants where Airbus&nbsp;
is now in the lead. And it has been for most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.980
the past 20 years, Europe's Airbus has recorded&nbsp;
more orders than its North American rival.
 &nbsp;

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:15.660
The question is, why? How is it possible that&nbsp;
the Europeans have managed to beat the mighty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.780
American commercial aviation industry?
 
Many of you may be thinking that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:23.700
change has a lot to do with the Boeing 737&nbsp;
MAX fiasco, a plane that was grounded for 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.320
months because air safety authorities banned&nbsp;
it from flying after two fatal crashes.
 &nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.520
And yes, in a way this has a lot to do&nbsp;
with what happened, after all, the 737&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.840
Max was Boeing's great hope. Although we are not&nbsp;
going to delve too much into this matter because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:41.760
we have already made a video entirely dedicated&nbsp;
to this topic here on VisualPolitik. I’ll leave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.640
the link at the end of the video.
 
But no, that's not the only reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.160
why Airbus has managed to overtake its US rival.&nbsp;
In this case, the story goes back much further.
 &nbsp;

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.280
Have you ever wondered how on earth a European&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:59.820
aircraft company managed to catch&nbsp;
up, in quality and technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.020
with the great American leader that controlled&nbsp;
this industry practically from the start?
 &nbsp;

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.580
How did Airbus come into being, whose&nbsp;
idea was it and why was it created,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.240
and who put up the money to make it look&nbsp;
like a rival for Boeing? And perhaps the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.260
most important question of all: What on earth&nbsp;
does all this have to do with VisualPolitik?
 &nbsp;

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:20.520
VisualPolitik viewers, the story of Airbus&nbsp;
and its war with Boeing is more than a story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:24.420
of competition between two companies, it&nbsp;
is also a story of political competition:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:29.400
the United States versus the European&nbsp;
Union. The old giant versus the new titan.
 &nbsp;

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:34.200
And believe me when I tell you that there are a&nbsp;
lot, and I mean a lot of layers in this one. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:40.020
is a story that goes far beyond aviation itself.&nbsp;
So... Are you ready? Are your seat belts fastened?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:48.120
Well... Let's get started.
 
(UNITY IS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:48.840
STRENGTH)
 &nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.640
The second half of the 20th century&nbsp;
was the era of economic explosion and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:01.080
modernity both in the West and in much of Asia.
 
And it was also the time when Europe was rising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:05.160
from the ashes of World War Two. Where once&nbsp;
there had been constant fighting and conflict,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.100
now suddenly there was agreement and cooperation.
 &nbsp;

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:12.780
In fact, if this had not been the case,&nbsp;
Airbus would probably never have existed.
 &nbsp;

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:22.560
The European aeronautics company par excellence&nbsp;
was launched in 1970. It was the result of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:26.700
political negotiation between governments&nbsp;
that had already begun a few years earlier.
 &nbsp;

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:30.180
Yes, that’s right. Airbus was not&nbsp;
the brainchild of some visionary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.200
entrepreneur or anything like that.
 
The idea came from the offices of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.160
French and West German governments&nbsp;
in the late 1960s. To achieve this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:43.020
these governments encouraged the union of the&nbsp;
French aeronautical company, Aerospatiale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:48.420
with the German company, Deutsche Airbus. The&nbsp;
consortium was later joined by aeronautical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.680
companies from Spain and the United Kingdom.
 
In essence, the idea was to replicate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:57.780
collaboration that the French and the British had&nbsp;
undertaken to create Concorde a few years earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.500
but in this case with the objective of&nbsp;
creating a long-term structure that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.520
make it possible to make aircraft that&nbsp;
were, shall we say, less exclusive.
 &nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.780
Aircraft that first had to be able to&nbsp;
fill a gap in which the American Boeing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:15.600
Lockheed and McDonnell Douglas, which completed&nbsp;
about 80% of the market, had no product. And later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.940
to compete with them, directly, on a large scale.
 
And so it is that the first aircraft to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:25.680
launched was the Airbus A300, which was the&nbsp;
first wide-body aircraft to make commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.500
flights with two engines under ETOPS standards.
 
But, let's not get sidetracked and let's get into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:35.940
the situation.
 
With the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.940 --> 00:04:40.320
Europeans manufacturing modern aircraft and&nbsp;
gradually gaining more and more market share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.420
thanks to the merger of several companies,&nbsp;
Boeing had to do something. So in 1997 it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:50.220
tried to replicate the model by acquiring&nbsp;
its direct US competitor, McDonnell Douglas.
 &nbsp;

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:54.360
However, with this purchase, the American&nbsp;
giant inherited some of the company’s problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:58.800
including economic and corporate culture problems&nbsp;
that would sooner or later take their toll.
 &nbsp;

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:05.820
Be that as it may, the fact is that the era of&nbsp;
what is perhaps the most important and powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:11.820
duopoly of all had begun: that of aviation.
 
Now, let's not go too fast. This is an industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:15.720
that requires a lot of capital investment,&nbsp;
that is, to make it work you have to put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:20.460
in a lot of money. As you can imagine, designing,&nbsp;
developing and manufacturing aircraft is not easy.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:25.500
The question is: Who was going to put up the money&nbsp;
to develop the technology that would allow each of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.660
the two companies to try to conquer the market?&nbsp;
For the first time, Boeing had a potential giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:35.760
in front of it, capable of looking at them eye to&nbsp;
eye, but how exactly would they compete? How did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.260
the Europeans intend to eclipse the US giant?
 
Well, you see, let me say it again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:45.240
in order to launch a new program, to create a&nbsp;
new airplane, two things are needed above all:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.680
the first is money, lots and lots of money.
 
But to give you a clearer idea of what we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:54.720
talking about, let's take a concrete example,&nbsp;
let's take a look at the case of Airbus A380,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.400
the world's largest airliner, which was&nbsp;
discontinued in March 2021 when the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:01.980
aircraft left the factory.
 
Well, take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:11.040
The A380 program, which includes the entire&nbsp;
development cycle from conceptual idea to entry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:16.860
into service, was initially estimated&nbsp;
in 2000 to cost about $11.3 billion.
 &nbsp;

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:20.520
However, estimates today point to the&nbsp;
development of this giant of the skies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:28.740
swallowing up more than $30 billion.
 
These are astronomical figures. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:33.960
keep in mind that Airbus itself acknowledges&nbsp;
that the A380 has never made a profit. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:38.640
it is not that it has not generated profits, but&nbsp;
that billions of dollars were lost along the way.
 &nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:42.300
And this is where we come to the&nbsp;
second thing you need to launch a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:46.200
new aircraft. In addition to money, there&nbsp;
obviously needs to be sufficient demand.
 &nbsp;

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.960
Given the numbers involved,&nbsp;
poor forecasts could turn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.120
development of the new aircraft into a disaster.
 
Well, right here we come to the most controversial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:59.820
part of this duel of titans. The question is,&nbsp;
what has Airbus done better than the Americans?
 &nbsp;

00:06:59.820 --> 00:07:04.620
From the point of view of many Americans, what&nbsp;
Europe has done with Airbus is nothing short of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:09.600
unfair competition. A situation that even provoked&nbsp;
a trade war between the United States and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.320
European bloc. Take a look.
 
(U.S. and EU Agree to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:17.580
Suspend Airbus-Boeing Trade Fight
 
The 17-year trade fight is the longest and most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:23.400
costly in the history of the WTO. U.S. importers&nbsp;
have paid more than $1.1 billion of tariffs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.400 --> 00:07:28.320
since the duties in the dispute took effect in&nbsp;
2019, according to data from U.S. Customs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:30.540
Border Protection. – Wall Street Journal)
 
Now, do you want to know exactly what this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:35.220
conflict is about and why it has occurred?&nbsp;
Has Airbus really grown thanks to favored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:39.000
treatment from European governments? Has this&nbsp;
been a case of unfair competition? And what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.040
about the United States?
 
Well, let's take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:53.760
(WAR OF EGOS)
 
The trade dispute between the United States and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.560
the European Union, and between Boeing and Airbus,&nbsp;
with the World Trade Organization in the middle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.960
had one very clear cause: financing.
 
You see, Airbus, the European aircraft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:08.340
manufacturer, has been receiving huge amounts&nbsp;
of public money since its inception to meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.340 --> 00:08:12.000
the costs of developing new aircraft.
 
But let's look at a few facts. Throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.700
the history of the consortium, the&nbsp;
governments of France, Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:19.560
the United Kingdom and Spain, later joined&nbsp;
by others, have injected tons of cash.

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:25.080
Money in the form of both direct subsidies and&nbsp;
loans at artificially low interest rates or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:28.440
directly, without any financial&nbsp;
cost, that is, without interest.
 &nbsp;

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.980
We are talking about aid that&nbsp;
could exceed 15 billion dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.100
but whose impact on the market&nbsp;
could, according to some estimates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:39.180
be in the region of 200 billion&nbsp;
dollars, all of which, logically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:43.980
works against Boeing in the United States.
 
All this funding is what is known as "launch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:48.540
aid" and forms one of the largest industrial&nbsp;
policy plans in Europe in recent decades.
 &nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.180
Basically, the European States used these tools&nbsp;
to cover a significant part of the cost of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:03.540
development of the first Airbus models, such as&nbsp;
the A300 or the mid-size aircraft family, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.540 --> 00:09:09.360
the A320, which competes with the Boeing 737.
 
The conflict arises because, initially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:14.580
the idea was that these subsidies would be reduced&nbsp;
until they disappeared as Airbus matured in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.860
market. And also because they would have to be&nbsp;
repaid in full as each program made a profit.
 &nbsp;

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:27.960
In other words, the company was to&nbsp;
be supported with European taxpayers'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:32.100
money as it took off with the expectation of&nbsp;
returning a higher profit down the track.
 &nbsp;

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:37.320
Basically, a typical industrial policy plan,&nbsp;
which I am sure many of you would consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:42.600
favorably. After all, Airbus was a success and,&nbsp;
well, you may also think that it is better to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:47.520
devote public resources to initiatives like this&nbsp;
one rather than to other types of... Let's say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:51.960
more abstract, less productive causes.
 
Of course, there would be a lot to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:56.640
about this matter, but that is more a topic&nbsp;
for our sister channel, VisualEconomik.
 &nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:04.500
In any case, as you can imagine, the Americans&nbsp;
are not happy about it. And even less so if we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:08.340
take into account that this financial&nbsp;
aid didn’t end up disappearing nor has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:13.320
Airbus repaid the totality of these loans.
 
(6 March 2019: Airbus In Dispute Over $600m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.980 --> 00:10:19.260
Of Unpaid Loans Used To Develop The A380
 
According to Reuters, Airbus claims that "it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:23.700
would no longer need to repay any outstanding&nbsp;
state loans on the A380 because governments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:26.880
had agreed to share risk in the roughly&nbsp;
15-billion-euro project." – Simple Flying)
 &nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:32.640
As you can see, these "launch aids" were so&nbsp;
advantageous that they apparently included a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:36.960
clause whereby, if the program went wrong,&nbsp;
the risk was shared between the lenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:42.240
that is governments, and the recipient: Airbus. In&nbsp;
other words, both would share the losses... Which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:46.920
by the way, does not happen with the profits.
 
In fact, it's not like Airbus is losing money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:52.500
Yes, the A380 program went badly, but the company&nbsp;
is going from strength to strength. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:59.520
in 2022, this company posted a net profit, that&nbsp;
means after tax, of more than 4.2 billion euros,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:03.780
the best result in its entire history.
 
Despite this, part of the losses on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:06.840
A380 program have been absorbed&nbsp;
by the European countries.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:09.900
[And to tell you the truth, I&nbsp;
don't know if this makes sense].
 &nbsp;

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:14.940
But what is clear is that the Americans don't&nbsp;
like it one bit. The anger is monumental.
 &nbsp;

00:11:14.940 --> 00:11:18.060
Both at Boeing headquarters and&nbsp;
among politicians in Washington.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:27.540
In their view, the Europeans have been torpedoing&nbsp;
their aircraft industry with alleged illegal aid.
 &nbsp;

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:32.220
And in fact, the World Trade Organization&nbsp;
went so far as to rule that such loans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.220 --> 00:11:36.240
Airbus generated unfair competition. So,&nbsp;
what can I say, they did have a point.
 &nbsp;

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:41.280
In order to make peace with Washington and&nbsp;
comply with WTO guidelines, the European Union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.440
France and Spain had to raise the interest&nbsp;
rates charged to Airbus for loans linked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:51.660
the launch of the A350, one of the European&nbsp;
manufacturer's new flagship programs.
 &nbsp;

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.900
Now then, stop, let’s put the brakes&nbsp;
on. Before you tip the scales in favor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.900 --> 00:11:59.100
of one or the other, wait a bit because&nbsp;
as we usually insist on VisualPolitik...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:03.420
every story has at least two versions.
 
And it’s not exactly as if the Americans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:07.500
have been behaving above board. Not at all.
 
In fact, we could even say that the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:11.520
government has bailed Boeing out of a lot of&nbsp;
trouble in recent years, and it has done so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:15.480
in a number of different ways. Some of them,&nbsp;
by the way, with catastrophic consequences.
 &nbsp;

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.020
Do you want to know what exactly we are talking&nbsp;
about? Do you want to know what tricks the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:27.780
Americans got up to? Well... Pay attention!
 
(BAILOUTS… UNDER THE TABLE?)

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:35.280
We have already told you that Airbus has received&nbsp;
a lot of money from European taxpayers. Debts that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:39.540
on many occasions have been forgiven, which&nbsp;
has clearly harmed Boeing in its competition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:44.640
with Airbus, but... What about the Americans?&nbsp;
Have they stood by with their arms folded?
 &nbsp;

00:12:44.640 --> 00:12:49.440
Well, obviously not. There are some winners&nbsp;
and losers in this story. Boeing has also been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:54.900
receiving billions of dollars in US government&nbsp;
funding and for many, huge sweetheart deals.
 &nbsp;

00:12:54.900 --> 00:12:58.440
Favorable treatment, which is reported&nbsp;
to have come at times from the US Federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:05.400
Aviation Agency itself, the until recently highly&nbsp;
respected FAA, the agency in charge of regulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:10.320
and overseeing all activities related to civil&nbsp;
aviation and its safety in the United States.
 &nbsp;

00:13:10.320 --> 00:13:15.900
The question is... How did they do it? Well, you&nbsp;
see, the way the United States has – and continues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:21.000
to – fund Boeing is through multi-billion&nbsp;
dollar military and aerospace contracts, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:26.760
many believe are awarded, with inflated prices.&nbsp;
In other words, as a kind of hidden subsidy.
 &nbsp;

00:13:26.760 --> 00:13:31.020
In fact, look at the data: Boeing's largest&nbsp;
contractors are none other than NASA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:34.740
the US Department of Defense and the&nbsp;
US Department of Homeland Security.
 &nbsp;

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:42.840
In 2017 alone Boeing earned $23.4 billion in US&nbsp;
government contracts, according to data compiled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:47.100
from the General Services Administration.
 
And in fact, in its defense, Airbus claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:50.100
that the constant and numerous&nbsp;
contracts Boeing receives from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:55.500
US government and public agencies are a way&nbsp;
of subsidizing the American aviation giant.
 &nbsp;

00:13:57.780 --> 00:14:03.000
But not only that, there is also the alleged&nbsp;
favorable treatment by the FAA that we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:09.060
telling you about a moment ago. Take a look.
 
(FAA saw high risk of crashes, but let Boeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:14.220
737 MAX keep flying – The Seattle Times)
 
([The Boeing 737 MAX crashes] "were the&nbsp;&nbsp;

