by the FAA" – Report of Transportation&nbsp;
and Infrastructure Committee of the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.700 --> 00:14:31.500
States House of Representatives)
 
(FAA and Boeing manipulated 737&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.500 --> 00:14:35.880
Max tests during recertification – The Verge)
 
In fact, all indications are that, in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:40.980
to the money that the United States injects into&nbsp;
Boeing every year through contracts, it was also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:45.960
helping the company by providing it with certain&nbsp;
laxity when it came to certifying its airplanes.
 &nbsp;

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:51.000
Specifically, the case of the 737 MAX, which&nbsp;
was an aircraft that was designed late and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:56.280
poorly to compete with the Airbus A320neo,&nbsp;
which was launched practically by surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.280 --> 00:15:00.540
and against which Boeing found itself&nbsp;
out of the market and unable to compete.
 &nbsp;

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:09.000
If these suspicions are confirmed, what we&nbsp;
can say is that the outcome was tragic.
 &nbsp;

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.684
But hold on because we haven't&nbsp;
told you about tax incentives yet.
 &nbsp;

00:15:11.684 --> 00:15:14.640
(12 March 2020: Washington state&nbsp;
repeals pro-Boeing tax break;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.640 --> 00:15:18.240
U.S. hopes to avoid EU tariffs
 
The changes would remove the 40%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:24.900
savings on Business and Occupation tax, which&nbsp;
saved Boeing some $118 million in 2018 based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:28.860
on published jetliner revenues. – Reuters)
 
Are these provisions not public subsidies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:33.120
that also harm free competition, and&nbsp;
are they not favoritism on the part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:36.420
US administrations towards Boeing?
 
Well... the truth is that the World&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:40.740
Trade Organization not only ruled against&nbsp;
Airbus, it also ruled against the Americans.
 &nbsp;

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:45.540
(WTO rules US failed to stop&nbsp;
unfair tax break to Boeing
 &nbsp;

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:51.180
The US had not enforced an order in 2012&nbsp;
to stop subsidies to the US aircraft maker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:55.980
through Washington state tax breaks. The undue&nbsp;
competitive advantage led to lost sales of Airbus'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.980 --> 00:16:01.500
A320neo and A320ceo single-aisle aircraft&nbsp;
in five sales campaigns. – Financial Times)
 &nbsp;

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:05.880
So, what can I say? Both giants are guilty of&nbsp;
doing the same thing but in different ways.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:11.220
This is a story in which there are neither good&nbsp;
guys nor bad guys... But at ground level there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:15.060
are big, "suckers": the taxpayers.
 
Because yes, it may be reasonable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.060 --> 00:16:18.360
to support the development of large&nbsp;
technological and industrial projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:22.980
but perhaps the aid should be fairer, limited&nbsp;
and open to more companies, don't you think?
 &nbsp;

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:27.780
The fact is that signing a trade peace agreement&nbsp;
on this issue between the United States and Europe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.780 --> 00:16:32.040
has not been easy, nor has it been something that&nbsp;
fell from the sky. What's more, there has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:37.080
one factor that has played a key role in the end&nbsp;
of the war between the two companies and the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:41.040
blocks. Can you guess what it could be?
 
Well... Listen up.
 &nbsp;

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:48.900
(WHAT CHINA HAS JOINED TOGETHER,&nbsp;
LET NO MAN PUT ASUNDER)
 &nbsp;

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:56.040
Seventeen years of war between the world's&nbsp;
two leading aircraft companies takes its toll.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:58.320
In the end it's something we&nbsp;
repeat a lot on VisualPolitik:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:03.540
trade wars don't usually benefit either side. And&nbsp;
the case of Boeing and Airbus is no different.
 &nbsp;

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:07.140
Both manufacturers have been hurting each&nbsp;
other... And by extension may have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.140 --> 00:17:10.680
hurting the development of the industry.
 
The fight between the United States and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:15.840
the European Union raised import and export costs,&nbsp;
restricted the use and development of technologies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:15.840 --> 00:17:20.520
and ultimately made aircraft more expensive&nbsp;
and slowed the evolution of the industry.
 &nbsp;

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:23.760
And do you know what? This created&nbsp;
an ideal breeding ground for others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.760 --> 00:17:27.660
to try to take advantage of the troubled&nbsp;
waters. Now the wolf's ears are in view.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:34.320
And yes, the wolf is, once again, China.
 
("We also agreed to work together [EU &amp; U.S.]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:38.700
to challenge and counter China's non-market&nbsp;
practices in this sector that give China's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:42.900
companies an unfair advantage." – Joe Biden)
 
As we also told you in a past video in our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:48.240
dedicated series of videos on the world of&nbsp;
aviation, the Airbus-Boeing duopoly could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:53.640
threatened, at least in the short and medium-haul&nbsp;
segment, by a new competitor: China's COMAC.
 &nbsp;

00:17:56.580 --> 00:18:00.420
Beijing is aggressively using a mix of&nbsp;
the two forms of public subsidies we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:04.680
have seen in this video, those used by the&nbsp;
Europeans and those used by the Americans.
 &nbsp;

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:09.720
The Chinese government is directly subsidizing&nbsp;
aircraft development, and is also placing huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:15.840
orders through its state-owned airlines.
 
In fact, the COMAC ARJ21, which can carry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:21.600
up to 100 passengers, is already flying&nbsp;
commercially, and the COMAC C919 is currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.180
on its initial commercial introduction.
 
Although it has encountered some problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.180 --> 00:18:27.360
and its entry into service may be&nbsp;
delayed from the 2023 schedule,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:32.700
the fact remains that this aircraft may pose a&nbsp;
huge threat to the future of Boeing and Airbus.
 &nbsp;

00:18:32.700 --> 00:18:37.500
We are talking about an aircraft that can carry&nbsp;
up to 174 passengers and therefore competes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.500 --> 00:18:44.400
directly with the A320 and 737 in China, the&nbsp;
flagships of the two Western giants. What's more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:49.680
China expects to manufacture 150 of&nbsp;
these aircraft per year by 2028.
 &nbsp;

00:18:52.500 --> 00:18:57.840
And this scenario, that of an aircraft&nbsp;
manufacturer for the Chinese short-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.840 --> 00:19:02.280
and medium-haul market, would be the&nbsp;
worst nightmare for Airbus and Boeing.
 &nbsp;

00:19:02.280 --> 00:19:06.660
Why? Because China is the key market for the&nbsp;
future of the two aviation giants. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:12.660
according to Boeing's own calculations, the Asian&nbsp;
giant will need to buy, wait for it, almost 8,500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:18.840
new passenger aircraft between now and 2041.
 
This is a real payday, and if a local competitor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:22.260
such as COMAC manages to take even&nbsp;
a significant slice of this pie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:26.760
the future of Boeing and Airbus could change&nbsp;
completely. A direct hit to the business.
 &nbsp;

00:19:26.760 --> 00:19:31.200
So both companies – and the governments of the&nbsp;
United States and the European Union – have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.200 --> 00:19:36.600
pragmatic. In recent years, both companies have&nbsp;
opened final assembly lines in China for some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:43.800
their most sought-after models, such as the&nbsp;
Boeing 737 or the Airbus A320 and A321neo.
 &nbsp;

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:48.660
In addition, the European Union has suggested to&nbsp;
the United States that they will end the so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:53.700
"launch aid" to Airbus, in exchange for financing&nbsp;
the consortium through military spending.
 &nbsp;

00:19:53.700 --> 00:20:00.000
Something we are already seeing. Check it out.
 
(Spain to lend Airbus 2.14 bln euros for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.640
military programmes – Reuters)
 
Yes, as many of you may be thinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:06.840
this is practically adopting the American&nbsp;
model of financing Boeing that Brussels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:10.560
has been fighting against for 17 years.
 
[Well, it’s not exactly unheard of for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:17.880
European politicians to change their tune...].
 
What is known so far is that both blocs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:22.740
the EU and the US, have signed a truce,&nbsp;
a ceasefire in this trade war, which in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:26.940
theory should be resolved before 2026.
 
The future? Well, we can't know for sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.940 --> 00:20:31.200
but what is clear is that neither Airbus&nbsp;
nor Boeing is in a position to complain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.200 --> 00:20:35.340
too much about unfair competition.
 
The two industrial giants have received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.340 --> 00:20:39.840
enormous amounts of money, both control&nbsp;
more or less a similar share of the market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:42.960
and both have enormous political&nbsp;
connections with the public sector.
 &nbsp;

00:20:42.960 --> 00:20:47.700
But now it's your turn: do you think Airbus and&nbsp;
Boeing will make peace for good? Should the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:47.700 --> 00:20:51.480
companies start working together to prevent the&nbsp;
Chinese from gaining a foothold in the market?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.480 --> 00:20:56.220
What do you think the war for control of the&nbsp;
skies may look like in the coming decades?
 &nbsp;

